{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 25, "model_name": "mistralai/mixtral-8x7b-instruct-v0.1", "content": " Raymond III of Tripoli was a Count of Tripoli who ruled from 1152 until his death in 1187 His reign was marked by conflict with the Zengid dynasty and the Kingdom of Jerusalem as well as the arrival of the Knights Templar and Hospitaller in the region He was the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem and was married twice first to Constance of Antioch and then to Eschiva of Bures With Constance he had two sons Bohemond III and Bohemond the Younger and a daughter Marie who married several prominent nobles in the region Raymond III became Count of Tripoli after his father's death in 1152 when he was only ten years old His mother Hodierna served as regent until he came of age In 1157 Raymond III allied with the Byzantine Empire against the Zengid ruler Nur ad-Din in an attempt to reclaim Edessa which had been lost to the Muslims in 1144 The campaign ultimately failed and Raymond III was captured and held prisoner for several years *Capture and imprisonment by Nur ad-Din (1157–1173)* During his imprisonment Raymond III's mother Hodierna died and his younger brother Bertrand ruled as regent Upon his release in 1173 Raymond III returned to Tripoli and resumed his rule He soon became embroiled in the conflict between the Zengids and the Kingdom of Jerusalem over the control of the city of Antioch In 1174 Raymond III allied with Bohemond III of Antioch and the Knights Templar and Hospitaller to defend the city against the Zengid forces However the allied forces were defeated and Raymond III was once again captured by the Zengids He was released in 1176 after agreeing to pay a large ransom *Conflict with the Zengids and the Kingdom of Jerusalem (1157–1187)* Raymond III's reign was also marked by the arrival of the Knights Templar and Hospitaller in the region In 1144 the Templars had established a headquarters in the Kingdom of Jerusalem and in 1147 the Hospitallers had followed suit The two orders played a significant role in the defense of the Crusader states in the region and often came into conflict with the local nobility including Raymond III The Knights Templar in particular were a source of tension as they were exempt from many of the feudal obligations that other nobles were subject to *Arrival of the Knights Templar and Hospitaller* Raymond III died in 1187 during the Battle of Hattin which was a major defeat for the Crusader forces against the Ayyubid sultan Saladin His death marked the end of the County of Tripoli as an independent state and its eventual incorporation into the Kingdom of Jerusalem.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -9.107174992095679e-05}, {"token": "III", "logprob": -0.0001829695247579366}, {"token": "of", "logprob": -1.0162993669509888}, {"token": "Tri", "logprob": -1.7165990357170813e-05}, {"token": "pol", "logprob": -2.3841830625315197e-06}, {"token": "i", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "was", "logprob": -0.10793045908212662}, {"token": "a", "logprob": -0.6054378151893616}, {"token": "Count", "logprob": -0.14736545085906982}, {"token": "of", "logprob": -8.106198947643861e-06}, {"token": "Tri", "logprob": -0.00012337400403339416}, {"token": "pol", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "i", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "who", "logprob": -0.8524313569068909}, {"token": "ruled", "logprob": -0.11309599131345749}, {"token": "from", "logprob": -0.005787164904177189}, {"token": "", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "5", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -0.00035232058144174516}, {"token": "until", "logprob": -0.011847606860101223}, {"token": "his", "logprob": -0.07889040559530258}, {"token": "death", "logprob": -3.218599158572033e-05}, {"token": "in", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "8", "log